  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nWhile chances for thunderstorms remain in the forecast into early\nnext week, there will be some differences in the broader setup which\ncould lead to changes to the overall potential for impacts. \n\nFor today and Friday, deep flow is primarily southwesterly with\nrich moisture advecting back into the entire Ohio Valley. Some\nshowers and a few thunderstorms are possible this afternoon, but the\nbetter chances arrive this evening and into the overnight as forcing\nincreases. Depending on the evolution of thunderstorms occurring over\nMissouri early this morning, a remnant MCV may eventually drift into\nthe area. By increasing low-level convergence, this could locally \nenhance thunderstorm development. Forecast soundings depict \nunidirectional southwesterly flow with moisture-rich profiles. \nTherefore, the primary threat with thunderstorm activity would be \nlocally heavy rainfall. The mid and upper trough is still in place \nthroughout the day on Friday, supporting showers and thunderstorms as\ninstability increases. Instability values are projected to be higher\non Friday, and that will support chances for a few strong storms. \n\nThe trough axis begins to shift eastward Friday night into Saturday\nmorning, and a weak boundary looks to move into the local area \nbefore stalling on Sunday. Lingering thunderstorm activity may be\nongoing into Saturday morning. A few showers and thunderstorms \ncannot be ruled out Saturday afternoon and Sunday, but there is an\nexpected lull due to the trough shifting eastward. \n\nLate Sunday and into early next week, deeper northwesterly flow\nbecomes established over the Great Lakes and Midwest. Low-level\nmoisture flow out of the southwest and steeper mid-level lapse rates\nsupport growing instability over the region. Opportunities for\norganized thunderstorm complexes will be possible in this broad\nwind flow and moisture regime, however, details are always quite \nuncertain at this range. The important item to note is that when\ncompared to the thunderstorms today and Friday, these thunderstorms \nwould pose a greater chance for damaging winds.",
